I mean, that's what he symbolises, is this break between Ireland and Britain. I mean, that's one of the things he signifies. But everything about him is surrounded by myth, obfuscation, and often error. I mean, Dominic's just mentioned, you know, probably the most famous words he ever mentioned in the name of God go. He never actually said them. I mean, you know, I mean, according to John Morrill's new collection, they seem to derive from a forged version of Cromwell's speeches. That's that's 1767 or something. Paul, next you'll be saying he never said warts and all.
我的意思是，这就是他所象征的，即爱尔兰和英国之间的这种决裂。我的意思是，这是他标志性的事情之一。但关于他的一切都被神话、混淆和常常是错误所包围。我的意思是，多米尼克刚才提到了，你知道，他可能说过最著名的话是“以上帝之名，走吧”。他实际上从未说过这话。我的意思是，你知道，根据约翰·莫雷尔的新合集，这些话似乎源自克伦威尔演讲的伪造版本。那是1767年左右的事。保罗，接下来你就要说他从未说过“warts and all”（全盘接受，不加掩饰）了。

85
00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:34.000
You know, there is so much about which we believe we know about Cromwell, but which we don't know. And this comes back to, you know, there's a terrible ignorance about Cromwell, for better or worse, on both sides of the Irish Sea. I mean, we all know Chesterton's old phrase about the difference between British and Irish history. The Irish can never forget it and the English can never remember it when we're talking about Irish history.
你知道，有关于克伦威尔，我们自以为知道但实际上并不知道的事情太多了。这就回到了——你知道，无论好坏，爱尔兰海两岸对克伦威尔都存在可怕的无知。我的意思是，我们都知道切斯特顿关于英国和爱尔兰历史区别的那句老话：爱尔兰人永远不会忘记，而英国人在谈论爱尔兰历史时却永远不会记得。

86
00:49:34.000 --> 00:50:22.000
But I think this is this absolutely crucial point in British, British and Irish, European, indeed even global history, is one that's still pretty much a blank slate as far as the vast majority of British people go, perhaps even Irish people. And it is something that I think really needs to be amended if we're to understand who we are. Paul, you often don't like modern parallels. And yet, I read in your book an excellent modern parallel, which I heartily approve of, where at the end of your great book, you say, you draw a parallel between Cromwell and Margaret Thatcher, both East Anglians, nonconformists, you know, very decisive when they needed to be very divisive.
但我认为这是英国、英国与爱尔兰、欧洲乃至全球历史中绝对关键的一点，就绝大多数英国人而言，它几乎还是一张白纸，或许对爱尔兰人来说也是如此。如果我们想了解我们是谁，我认为这确实是需要修正的一点。保罗，你通常不喜欢现代类比。然而，我在你的书里读到一个极好的现代类比，我对此非常赞赏，在你的巨著结尾，你将克伦威尔与玛格丽特·撒切尔进行了类比，两人都是东盎格利亚人、不信奉国教者，你知道，在需要的时候非常果断，也非常具分裂性。

87
00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:52.000
Yeah, thought of as austere, but actually incredibly fun-loving. Do you think that, so talk to us about the Margaret Thatcher analogy, because I think that's one that most listeners will find extraordinary. Well, I was being quite mischievous when I did this. Obviously, it is absolutely true that I don't like to draw modern parallels too closely, but I couldn't miss the Margaret Thatcher one, because, you know, as you say, there's this East Anglian non-conformist philo-semite. We forget Cromwell's role
是的，被认为严于律己，但实际上极其热爱生活。你认为——所以跟我们谈谈玛格丽特·撒切尔的类比吧，因为我认为大多数听众会觉得这个类比非同寻常。嗯，我当时写这部分时其实挺恶作剧的。很显然，我确实不喜欢把现代类比拉得太近，但我不能错过玛格丽特·撒切尔这个类比，因为正如你所说，这里有一个东盎格利亚非信奉国教的犹太人支持者。我们忘记了克伦威尔

88
00:50:52.000 --> 00:51:21.000
in resettling Jews in England again. Not that he did this for humanitarian purposes. He did it so that the Jews would convert to Protestantism, Christianity, in the most perfect of all Christian polities. But decisive in war, certainly. Problem with the Irish, that's certainly there as well. There are all kinds of parallels. I was being quite facetious, but I think the most interesting parallel of all, and we talked earlier about the Anglicisation
在让犹太人重新在英格兰定居方面所扮演的角色。并不是说他是出于人道主义目的才这么做的。他这么做是为了让犹太人皈依新教、皈依基督教，从而在最完美的基督教政体中实现这一点。但在战争中当然是果断的。在爱尔兰问题上也有麻烦，这当然也存在。有各种各样的类比。我当时写得很俏皮，但我认为最有趣的类比是，我们之前讨论过

89
00:51:21.000 --> 00:52:03.000
of both Scotland and Ireland that was the plan of this. But I think in the end, even the English people disappointed Cromwell. He described them, and I love this Cromwellian phrase, as under circumcision but raw. In other words, they were on a kind of path to a holiness, a perfection, but they weren't there yet. And indeed, they were a long way off. And I think there was that kind of element with Thatcher. There's a wonderful phrase, I can't remember who did it, I don't know whether it was Vernon Bogden or someone else, who said that Margaret Thatcher wanted to create a country in the image of her father, the Puritan Alderman Roberts,
苏格兰和爱尔兰的盎格鲁化，这是当时的计划。但我认为到头来，连英国人民都让克伦威尔失望了。他形容他们——我喜欢这个克伦威尔式的短语——“尚未受割礼但却生疏”。换句话说，他们走在通往圣洁、完美的道路上，但他们还没到那一步。事实上，他们离那还很远。我认为撒切尔夫人身上也有这种成分。有一个绝妙的短语，我不记得是谁说的了，我不知道是弗农·博格丹还是别的什么人，他说玛格丽特·撒切尔想按照她父亲（清教徒市议员罗伯茨）的形象

